  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper investigates whether, upon bacterial cell lysis in nutrient-limited environments, released E. coli Lon protease degrades co-liberated proteins into small peptides that surviving E. coli can import and metabolise, thereby supporting post-mortem nutrient recycling.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When nutrient limitation makes macromolecule catabolism and nutrient import important, evidence that a released protease converts lysed-cell proteins into usable peptide nutrients supports studying that process as nutrient recycling."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Enzyme-degraded protein nutrients, exemplified by casein hydrolysate, can enhance wild-type E. coli growth in minimal media by providing an accessible nutrient form."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Because bacteria import small peptides rather than whole proteins, proteins released during bacterial cell lysis would need to be degraded into small peptides before they can support growth of surviving cells."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) The growth support from wild-type E. coli lysate, together with the failure of Lon-null lysate to support growth, implicates released Lon protease in processing co-liberated proteins into small peptide nutrients usable by surviving E. coli."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) These findings motivate investigating Lon-mediated degradation of proteins released by dead E. coli cells as a post-mortem nutrient-recycling mechanism relevant to nutrient-limited bacterial environments."];
